**File: `srs.md`**

# 1. Introduction
### 1.1 Purpose
The purpose of this Software Requirements Specification (SRS) is to define, in a clear and traceable manner, the functional and non‑functional requirements for the **Order Management System (OMS)**. The document serves as the definitive reference for designers, developers, testers, and stakeholders.

### 1.2 Scope
The OMS enables customers to browse products, place orders, and make payments; order staff to receive, fulfil, ship, and close orders; and accountants to raise invoices and confirm or reject payments. The system stores and manages customers, products, orders, invoices, and payments while enforcing business rules and security constraints.

### 1.3 Definitions and Acronyms
| Term | Definition |
|------|------------|
| **Customer** | External user who purchases products. |
| **OrderStaff** | Internal staff responsible for order processing. |
| **Accountant** | Internal staff responsible for invoicing and payment handling. |
| **Order** | A request submitted by a Customer that contains one or more OrderItems. |
| **Invoice** | A billing document generated for an Order. |
| **Payment** | A monetary transaction made against an Invoice. |
| **Status** | The current lifecycle state of an Order, Invoice, or Payment. |
| **PaymentMethod** | The channel used for payment – *bank*, *card*, or *cash*. |
| **NFR** | Non‑functional requirement. |
| **US** | User story (functional requirement). |

# 2. Overall description
### 2.1 Product perspective
The OMS is a standalone web‑based application that persists data in a relational database. It interacts with external services only for TLS‑protected communication and optional payment gateways.

### 2.2 Product functions
* Manage customers, products, and bank accounts.  
* Allow customers to submit orders containing order items.  
* Enable order staff to receive, validate, fill, ship, and close orders.  
* Allow accountants to raise invoices, record payments, and confirm or reject payments.  
* Enforce business rules concerning statuses, multiplicities, and permitted values.  

### 2.3 User characteristics
| Role | Typical expertise |
|------|-------------------|
| Customer | Basic web navigation, no technical knowledge required. |
| OrderStaff | Familiar with order processing workflows; basic data entry skills. |
| Accountant | Familiar with invoicing and payment reconciliation; understands status lifecycles. |

### 2.4 Assumptions and dependencies
* The system runs on a server that supports Python 3.12 and a PostgreSQL database.  
* TLS 1.2+ is available for all network communication.  
* Users are authenticated by an external identity provider (not in scope).  

# 3. Requirements
## 3.1 Functional requirements
| ID | User story | Acceptance criteria |
|----|------------|----------------------|
| **US-01** | As a **Customer**, I want to register my personal data (name, address, phone) so that the system can identify me for future orders. | **US-01-AC01** (condition) – All three attributes are mandatory and stored as non‑empty strings. <br> **US-01-AC02** (condition) – The system rejects a registration if any mandatory attribute is missing. <br> **US-01-AC03** (given_when_then) – Given a valid registration request, When the system processes it, Then a new Customer record is created and the user receives a confirmation. |
| **US-02** | As a **Customer**, I want to optionally add a single bank account so that I can pay by bank transfer. | **US-02-AC01** (condition) – At most one BankAccount may be linked to a Customer. <br> **US-02-AC02** (condition) – The bank account fields (accountNumber, routingNumber) are mandatory when a BankAccount is provided. <br> **US-02-AC03** (given_when_then) – Given a Customer with no BankAccount, When the Customer adds a bank account, Then the account is stored and linked to the Customer. |
| **US-03** | As a **Customer**, I want to browse products that have a name, description, price and image so that I can decide what to buy. | **US-03-AC01** (condition) – All four attributes are mandatory; price is a decimal. <br> **US-03-AC02** (condition) – The system rejects a Product creation if any mandatory attribute is missing. <br> **US-03-AC03** (given_when_then) – Given a valid Product, When the product is stored, Then it becomes searchable by all users. |
| **US-04** | As a **Customer**, I want to submit an order that contains at least one order item referencing existing products so that I can purchase them. | **US-04-AC01** (condition) – An Order must reference exactly one existing Customer. <br> **US-04-AC02** (condition) – Each OrderItem must reference exactly one existing Product. <br> **US-04-AC03** (given_when_then) – Given a known Customer and existing Products, When the Customer submits the order, Then the Order is stored with status *submitted* and the total amount is computed as Σ(quantity × unitPrice). |
| **US-05** | As a **Customer**, I want the system to reject my order if the Customer is unknown, the order has no items, or any referenced product does not exist, so that only valid orders are recorded. | **US-05-AC01** (given_when_then) – Given an unknown Customer, When an order is submitted, Then the system returns an error and no Order is created. <br> **US-05-AC02** (given_when_then) – Given an order with zero OrderItems, When the order is submitted, Then the system returns an error and no Order is created. <br> **US-05-AC03** (given_when_then) – Given an OrderItem that references a non‑existent Product, When the order is submitted, Then the system returns an error and no Order is created. |
| **US-06** | As an **OrderStaff**, I want to receive a submitted order so that it moves to *pending* and can be processed. | **US-06-AC01** (condition) – Status transition *submitted* → *pending* is allowed. <br> **US-06-AC02** (given_when_then) – Given an Order in *submitted* status, When OrderStaff selects *Receive*, Then the Order status becomes *pending*. <br> **US-06-AC03** (given_when_then) – Given an Order not in *submitted* status, When OrderStaff attempts *Receive*, Then the system rejects the request and the status remains unchanged. |
| **US-07** | As an **OrderStaff**, I want to mark a pending order as filled so that it can be shipped later. | **US-07-AC01** (condition) – Status transition *pending* → *filled* is allowed. <br> **US-07-AC02** (given_when_then) – Given an Order in *pending* status, When *Mark Filled* is executed, Then the Order status becomes *filled*. <br> **US-07-AC03** (given_when_then) – Given an Order not in *pending* status, When *Mark Filled* is attempted, Then the request is rejected and the status stays unchanged. |
| **US-08** | As an **OrderStaff**, I want to decline a pending order so that it becomes *closed* and no further processing occurs. | **US-08-AC01** (condition) – Status transition *pending* → *closed* is allowed. <br> **US-08-AC02** (given_when_then) – Given an Order in *pending* status, When *Decline* is chosen, Then the Order status becomes *closed*. <br> **US-08-AC03** (given_when_then) – Given an Order not in *pending* status, When *Decline* is attempted, Then the request is rejected. |
| **US-09** | As an **OrderStaff**, I want to ship a filled order so that its status becomes *shipped*. | **US-09-AC01** (condition) – Status transition *filled* → *shipped* is allowed. <br> **US-09-AC02** (given_when_then) – Given an Order in *filled* status, When *Ship* is executed, Then the Order status becomes *shipped*. <br> **US-09-AC03** (given_when_then) – Given an Order not in *filled* status, When *Ship* is attempted, Then the request is rejected. |
| **US-10** | As an **Accountant**, I want to raise an invoice for a filled or shipped order that has no invoice yet so that the customer can be billed. | **US-10-AC01** (condition) – An Invoice may be created only for Orders with status *filled* or *shipped* and only if no Invoice exists for that Order. <br> **US-10-AC02** (condition) – Invoice status is initially *issued*. <br> **US-10-AC03** (given_when_then) – Given a qualifying Order, When the Accountant creates an Invoice with date, amount, payment method, Then an Invoice record linked to the Order is stored with status *issued*. |
| **US-11** | As an **Accountant**, I want the system to reject attempts to raise a second invoice for the same order or to raise an invoice for an order whose status is *submitted*, *pending* or *closed*. | **US-11-AC01** (given_when_then) – Given an Order that already has an Invoice, When the Accountant tries to raise another Invoice, Then the request is rejected. <br> **US-11-AC02** (given_when_then) – Given an Order in *submitted*, *pending* or *closed* status, When the Accountant tries to raise an Invoice, Then the request is rejected. |
| **US-12** | As a **Customer**, I want to submit a payment against an *issued* invoice so that the invoice can be settled. | **US-12-AC01** (condition) – Payment may be created only for Invoices with status *issued*. <br> **US-12-AC02** (condition) – New Payment status is *pending*. <br> **US-12-AC03** (given_when_then) – Given an *issued* Invoice, When the Customer submits a Payment (date, amount, method), Then a Payment record linked to the Invoice is stored with status *pending*. |
| **US-13** | As a **Customer**, I want the system to reject a payment against a *paid* invoice. | **US-13-AC01** (given_when_then) – Given an Invoice with status *paid*, When the Customer attempts to submit a Payment, Then the request is rejected. |
| **US-14** | As a **Customer**, I want to submit a further payment if a previous payment failed, without losing the failed record. | **US-14-AC01** (condition) – A new Payment may be created for an Invoice that already has a Payment with status *failed*. <br> **US-14-AC02** (given_when_then) – Given an Invoice with a *failed* Payment, When the Customer submits another Payment, Then a new Payment record with status *pending* is created while the failed record remains unchanged. |
| **US-15** | As an **Accountant**, I want to confirm a pending payment so that the payment becomes *completed* and the related invoice becomes *paid*. | **US-15-AC01** (condition) – Payment status transition *pending* → *completed* is allowed. <br> **US-15-AC02** (condition) – When a Payment for an *issued* Invoice is confirmed, the Invoice status changes to *paid*. <br> **US-15-AC03** (given_when_then) – Given a *pending* Payment, When the Accountant confirms it, Then the Payment status becomes *completed* and the linked Invoice status becomes *paid*. |
| **US-16** | As an **Accountant**, I want to reject a pending payment so that the payment status becomes *failed* while the invoice and order remain unchanged. | **US-16-AC01** (condition) – Payment status transition *pending* → *failed* is allowed. <br> **US-16-AC02** (given_when_then) – Given a *pending* Payment, When the Accountant rejects it, Then the Payment status becomes *failed* and the Invoice and Order statuses stay as they were. |
| **US-17** | As an **Accountant**, I want the system to reject confirmation or rejection of a payment that is not *pending*. | **US-17-AC01** (given_when_then) – Given a Payment with status *completed* or *failed*, When the Accountant attempts to confirm or reject it, Then the request is rejected and the status remains unchanged. |
| **US-18** | As an **OrderStaff**, I want to close a shipped order whose invoice is paid so that the order lifecycle ends. | **US-18-AC01** (condition) – Status transition *shipped* → *closed* is allowed only if the linked Invoice status is *paid*. <br> **US-18-AC02** (given_when_then) – Given an Order in *shipped* with a *paid* Invoice, When *Close* is executed, Then the Order status becomes *closed*. |
| **US-19** | As an **OrderStaff**, I want the system to reject closing a shipped order whose invoice is not paid, or closing an order whose invoice is paid but whose order status is not shipped. | **US-19-AC01** (given_when_then) – Given an Order in *shipped* with an Invoice not *paid*, When *Close* is attempted, Then the request is rejected. <br> **US-19-AC02** (given_when_then) – Given an Order with a *paid* Invoice but status not *shipped*, When *Close* is attempted, Then the request is rejected. |
| **US-20** | As any user, I want the system to prevent any status change on a *closed* order, invoice, or payment. | **US-20-AC01** (condition) – Once an entity reaches *closed* (Order) or *paid* (Invoice) or *completed*/*failed* (Payment), its status is immutable. <br> **US-20-AC02** (given_when_then) – Given a *closed* Order, When any user attempts a status transition, Then the request is rejected. |
| **US-21** | As a **System**, I want every attribute (except optional bank account) to be mandatory and validated on creation so that data quality is guaranteed. | **US-21-AC01** (condition) – All mandatory attributes must be non‑empty and of correct type (e.g., decimal for monetary values). <br> **US-21-AC02** (given_when_then) – Given a creation request missing any mandatory attribute, When the request is processed, Then the system rejects it with an error. |
| **US-22** | As a **System**, I want monetary values to be stored as decimal numbers to avoid rounding errors. | **US-22-AC01** (condition) – Price, unit price, total amount, invoice amount, and payment amount are stored using a decimal type with at least two fractional digits. |
| **US-23** | As a **System**, I want the allowed status values and payment methods to be restricted to the enumerated sets defined in the domain. | **US-23-AC01** (condition) – Order status ∈ {submitted, pending, filled, shipped, closed}. <br> **US-23-AC02** (condition) – Invoice status ∈ {issued, paid}. <br> **US-23-AC03** (condition) – Payment status ∈ {pending, completed, failed}. <br> **US-23-AC04** (condition) – Payment method ∈ {bank, card, cash}. |
| **US-24** | As a **System**, I want status transitions to be limited to the allowed sequences so that the lifecycle is predictable. | **US-24-AC01** (condition) – Order: submitted → pending → filled → shipped → closed (or submitted → pending → closed). <br> **US-24-AC02** (condition) – Invoice: issued → paid. <br> **US-24-AC03** (condition) – Payment: pending → completed OR pending → failed. |

*The above 24 user stories together realise all 50 functional domain requirements. Acceptance criteria reference the specific rules (e.g., multiplicities, status changes) that were originally expressed as REQ‑F‑xx.*

## 3.2 Quality attributes
### 3.2.1 Performance
| ID | Statement |
|----|-----------|
| **NFR-01** | **REQ‑N‑01** When a user submits an order containing up to 50 order items under normal operating conditions, the system shall register the order and respond within 2 seconds for at least 95 % of submissions. |
| **NFR-02** | **REQ‑N‑02** Under normal operating conditions, the system shall support at least 200 concurrent users and at least 20 order submissions per second while meeting the response time of NFR‑01. |

### 3.2.2 Security
| ID | Statement |
|----|-----------|
| **NFR-03** | **REQ‑N‑05** The system shall authenticate every user before granting access to any customer, order, invoice or payment data, and shall reject 100 % of unauthenticated requests. |
| **NFR-04** | **REQ‑N‑06** The system shall allow only users holding the accountant role to confirm or reject a payment, and shall reject the same request from any other user. |
| **NFR-05** | **REQ‑N‑07** The system shall protect customer personal data and bank account numbers by encrypting them in transit with TLS 1.2 or higher and at rest. |
| **NFR-06** | **REQ‑N‑08** The system shall record the user and the time of every status change of an order, an invoice and a payment, and shall retain these records for at least 12 months. |

### 3.2.3 Availability
| ID | Statement |
|----|-----------|
| **NFR-07** | **REQ‑N‑03** The system shall be available for at least 99.5 % of each calendar month, excluding scheduled maintenance announced at least 24 hours in advance. |
| **NFR-08** | **REQ‑N‑04** When a server failure occurs, the system shall resume accepting requests within 15 minutes without losing any order, invoice or payment recorded before the failure. |

# 4. Verification
Verification of each functional requirement shall be performed by automated unit and integration tests that exercise the given‑when‑then scenarios and validate the condition constraints. Non‑functional requirements shall be verified by performance load testing, security penetration testing, and availability monitoring as described in the test plan.

# 5. Supporting information
* Glossary of terms (see Clause 1.3).  
* References to regulatory standards for data protection (e.g., GDPR).  
* Architecture diagrams (see `structural.puml` and `behavioral.puml`).  

---  
*All identifiers used in this document correspond to entries in `requirements.json` and traceability matrix `VMX.csv`.*  



